WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.700
Glad you're with us on first principles daily, episode 52. Starting the week by asking what

00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:12.800
things should actually cost. A prosthetic limb build at $50,000 contains roughly $50 of plastic

00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:19.300
and actuators. The gap lives in custom labor and process, not the materials. Consider a finished

00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:25.340
below knee prosthesis. It can reach a build price of $50,000 while the thermoplastics,

00:00:25.340 --> 00:00:32.940
carbon fiber reinforcement, fasteners, and basic actuators inside it total perhaps $50 at commodity rates.

00:00:33.720 --> 00:00:40.180
That spread is not explained by exotic alloys or rare semiconductors. It traces instead to how

00:00:40.180 --> 00:00:46.020
the socket is shaped, how the alignment is tuned, and how reimbursement flows through a low volume

00:00:46.020 --> 00:00:52.020
clinical channel. The same pattern appears in simpler body-powered hands that children outgrow

00:00:52.020 --> 00:00:58.380
every year. The parts themselves are inexpensive yet each new device still triggers a full custom

00:00:58.380 --> 00:01:04.060
workflow. The surprise is that the physics of supporting body weight or transmitting grip force

00:01:04.060 --> 00:01:10.740
has not changed. Only the sequence of steps required to turn raw polymer into a device matched to one

00:01:10.740 --> 00:01:17.680
person has stayed locked in place. Residual limb geometry, skin condition, activity level, and socket

00:01:17.680 --> 00:01:23.540
interface pressure all differ from patient to patient. A prosthetist therefore takes measurements

00:01:23.540 --> 00:01:30.420
or a cast, then carves or prints a test socket. The prosthetist iterates through multiple fittings and

00:01:30.420 --> 00:01:36.740
finally delivers a definitive device whose alignment must be adjusted under load. Each of those steps

00:01:36.740 --> 00:01:43.620
requires a licensed clinician whose time is reimbursed at professional rates. The entire sequence repeats

00:01:43.620 --> 00:01:50.500
whenever the patient grows, loses weight or needs a new foot module. Component suppliers sell feet,

00:01:50.860 --> 00:01:57.240
knees, and liners in small batches because annual demand for any single size or stiffness remains modest.

00:01:57.960 --> 00:02:03.140
The resulting unit prices already embed low volume machining or molding overhead.

00:02:03.820 --> 00:02:09.720
Regulatory pathways for new socket materials or myoelectric controllers add documentation and

00:02:09.720 --> 00:02:16.140
testing costs that are amortized across few units. Insurance and Medicare reimbursement schedules

00:02:16.140 --> 00:02:22.760
further lock the workflow in place. Payment is tied to documented clinical encounters rather than

00:02:22.760 --> 00:02:27.900
to the delivered hardware alone, so practices have little incentive to collapse steps.

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:33.780
The net result is that a device whose raw materials could be purchased for the price of a few kilograms

00:02:33.780 --> 00:02:39.680
of polypropylene and a sheet of carbon fiber prepreg ends up carrying professional service margins,

00:02:40.360 --> 00:02:45.920
repeated office visits, and fragmented supply chain markups. Those markups together multiply

00:02:45.920 --> 00:02:52.300
the price by several hundred times. One objection often raised is that every patient truly needs

00:02:52.300 --> 00:02:59.140
individualized attention. Yet the same individualized attention is applied even to simple body-powered

00:02:59.140 --> 00:03:05.640
devices whose geometry changes mainly with growth. This suggests the process itself has become the

00:03:05.640 --> 00:03:12.180
default rather than a response to irreducible variation. Another factor is the carrying cost of

00:03:12.180 --> 00:03:18.040
inventory. A clinic cannot afford to stock every possible socket shape or foot stiffness,

00:03:18.600 --> 00:03:24.740
so each order waits for fabrication and shipping. That weight adds weeks during which capital is tied

00:03:24.740 --> 00:03:31.540
up and patient mobility remains limited. A rough magic wand estimate begins with the major constituents

00:03:31.540 --> 00:03:39.120
of a typical transdivial prosthesis. The socket itself might use two kilograms of polypropylene or

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:45.840
a similar thermoplastic at roughly two dollars per kilogram for four dollars total. A carbon fiber

00:03:45.840 --> 00:03:52.680
foot plate or keel adds perhaps eight dollars of composite material. Liners, suspension sleeves,

00:03:52.680 --> 00:03:59.480
and basic hardware, pyramid adapters, bolts, and a simple ankle block contribute another 20 to 30

00:03:59.480 --> 00:04:06.020
dollars at wholesale commodity prices. Even if one includes a modest actuator or microprocessor

00:04:06.020 --> 00:04:12.720
board for an advanced knee, the total raw material value remains on the order of 50 to 100 dollars.

00:04:13.380 --> 00:04:18.860
Against the finished, fitted, and build price that commonly reaches 30 to 60 thousand dollars,

00:04:18.860 --> 00:04:26.680
the idiot index therefore sits between 300 and 1000. That ratio does not measure material waste.

00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:33.700
It measures the accumulated cost of custom geometric capture, iterative fitting labor, low volume

00:04:33.700 --> 00:04:39.820
component fabrication, regulatory overhead, and the financing carry that accrues while a patient

00:04:39.820 --> 00:04:46.300
waits weeks for each revision. To see the arithmetic another way, divide the build price by the

00:04:46.300 --> 00:04:54.220
material floor. 50,000 divided by 50 yields 1000, meaning every dollar of polymer or fiber is

00:04:54.220 --> 00:04:59.300
multiplied a thousand fold by the sequence of human and administrative steps that follow.

00:04:59.960 --> 00:05:07.120
The open source EE Aenable designs illustrate the same arithmetic at smaller scale. A 3D printed

00:05:07.120 --> 00:05:13.380
body-powered hand can be produced for under 50 dollars in filament and fasteners. Yet commercial

00:05:13.380 --> 00:05:18.820
equivalents still list for several thousand because the clinical delivery channel has not changed.

00:05:19.400 --> 00:05:25.680
One might object that the magic wand figure ignores the value of skilled judgment. Yet the index

00:05:25.680 --> 00:05:31.980
itself already isolates that judgment as the dominant term, because the material cost is fixed

00:05:31.980 --> 00:05:37.780
and the remainder is everything else required to deliver the device. A redesign would first

00:05:37.780 --> 00:05:43.920
separate the geometric capture step from the physical fabrication step. Parametric software that

00:05:43.920 --> 00:05:49.600
converts a phone scan or simple pressure map into a printable socket file could eliminate the need

00:05:49.600 --> 00:05:56.060
for a plaster cast and multiple carved test sockets. The patient would receive an initial printed

00:05:56.060 --> 00:06:02.440
check socket within hours rather than days. Clinics could stock families of modular adjustable

00:06:02.440 --> 00:06:07.980
pylons and foot modules whose alignment is tuned with standard wrenches instead of custom

00:06:07.980 --> 00:06:14.840
machine adapters. That approach would cut the part count and lead time burden. Reimbursement could

00:06:14.840 --> 00:06:20.600
be unbundled so that fitting expertise is paid for directly while the printed socket and off the

00:06:20.600 --> 00:06:27.440
shelf modules are sourced at nearer to material cost. These moves become viable only when scanned

00:06:27.440 --> 00:06:33.660
to print accuracy is validated across body types. They also require that durable printable

00:06:33.660 --> 00:06:40.440
resins meet ISO fatigue standards for load bearing sockets. Regulatory language must recognize

00:06:40.440 --> 00:06:47.020
digitally derived sockets as equivalent to traditionally molded ones. The hard limits remain real.

00:06:47.560 --> 00:06:54.020
Serious amputations still require a clinician to assess tissue viability, manage volume fluctuation,

00:06:54.020 --> 00:07:01.120
and verify gate safety under load. Myoelectric signal processing and battery life have not yet reached

00:07:01.120 --> 00:07:07.700
the reliability patient's need for daily heavy use. Children or very active adults will continue

00:07:07.700 --> 00:07:14.320
to need frequent replacements regardless of fabrication speed. The prize is therefore concentrated in

00:07:14.320 --> 00:07:20.320
the subset of devices where the dominant cost today is repeated custom labor rather than unsolved

00:07:20.320 --> 00:07:27.300
control or materials problems. Body-powered arms for growing children and passive or semi-active

00:07:27.300 --> 00:07:33.960
lower limb sockets for moderate activity fall into this category. A further objection is that digital

00:07:33.960 --> 00:07:40.440
sockets might fail under unexpected loads. Addressing that requires published fatigue data on the

00:07:40.440 --> 00:07:47.100
specific printable resins under cyclic compression, not merely faster printing. When every new device

00:07:47.100 --> 00:07:52.660
restarts a full measurement to fitting cycle, the dominant expense is the repeated translation of

00:07:52.660 --> 00:07:59.180
anatomy into hardware rather than the hardware itself. When fitting expertise is treated as a scarce

00:07:59.180 --> 00:08:05.480
clinical service instead of a software parameter, the price stays anchored to professional hours instead of

00:08:05.480 --> 00:08:11.440
to kilograms of polymer. The first concrete signal that someone is attacking the gap will be a clinic

00:08:11.440 --> 00:08:17.380
publishing validated scan to print socket outcomes alongside reduced build cost for the same

00:08:17.380 --> 00:08:23.220
patient population. The second will be an insurer reimbursing a digitally delivered socket at a line

00:08:23.220 --> 00:08:29.680
item closer to material cost while still paying separately for the prosthetists verification visit.

00:08:30.260 --> 00:08:35.040
Who could do this and what would be the first concrete signal that someone is actually trying?

00:08:35.680 --> 00:08:40.660
That's first principle's daily for today. If this changed how you see what things cost,

00:08:40.660 --> 00:08:46.180
share it with someone who'd appreciate it and subscribe wherever you listen. I'm Patrick and

00:08:46.180 --> 00:08:52.460
Vancouver, one example or one opportunity every day. See you tomorrow. And if you'd rather watch

